A||||50||fiero56||clutch line ATF||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||01-05-2000||09:54 PM||||Well guys, I now have 1,000 miles on my clutch job. When I replaced the master and slave cylinders many people were recommending a switch from DOT3 to ATF. After I added the ATF many people commented on how the petroleum base of ATF will expand the seals on the master and slave cylinders. At first my clutch pedal feel was normal. But with in the last week I notice that more force is required to push the clutch pedal down, and the pedal does not rise back up very quickly. I can very easily lift my foot faster than the pedal will rise. I can only attribute this to the seals expanding and not sliding in the cylinder as easily. I'm going to be ordering new seals for both cylinders and see if the problem goes away. I would only recommend adding ATF to your clutch hydraulic system as a last resort before you buy new master and slave cylinders. <P>Anyone else notice this problem? ATF users, what type of climates do you live in. I notice pedal feel gets lighter as outside temp. rise.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick||01-06-2000||03:44 AM||||<BR>Although I live in Canada, the city of Vancouver can hardly be considered part of the frozen north. It's usually quite mild here, even in the winter. The coldest it's gotten so far this winter is just down to the freezing point. So in answer to your question...<P><B>My clutch with ATF continues to work perfectly.</B><P>I have not noticed your problem at all. ||||reg||14
Z||000002||Alex4mula||01-06-2000||09:24 AM||||I live in sunny Florida. Bought two new cylinders and put the ATF. After 5 days got exactly what you are experiencing. I had the problem in both, slave and master. Replaced both again and put Synthetic brake fluid and works perfectly and smooth now. Others have been lucky. I'm glad to hear that it was not only me...||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||01-06-2000||12:54 PM||||Not a single place in town has the rebuilds. I ordered from the Fiero Store. $80 for mas. and slav. rebuilds. Guess I'm stuck waiting for them to come in, and have to suffer with a very poorly performing clutch system until they arrive.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-06-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000004||william_thorniii||01-06-2000||01:40 PM||||56,<P>Did you try Genuine Auto?<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000005||william_thorniii||01-06-2000||01:40 PM||||56,<P>Did you try Genuine Auto?<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000007||fiero56||01-06-2000||02:06 PM||||Acutally, the nearest napa to me is all the way up in Moraine. And, right now, again, the Fiero is up on jackstands. While on the I-75 Third Street exit ramp(on my way to class), my brakes promplty failed. I was just starting to push the pedal down, no resistance. Pedal just went dead to the floor, nothing(this is at 75mph on a very short, downhill exit ramp). After two attemps at slowing down the brakes grabbed, And actually worked. But I had to push the pedal nearly to the floor. I crept up to school, into the parking garage, and checked my brake system out. The right rear caliper is shot. I have to buy a rebuild for it also. There were no warning signs. I drove to the gas station, drove around town it was perfect, until 12 miles later on the highway.<BR>Everyone is out doing their thing, my classes got cancelled today, so I'm at home waiting for my brother to come home so I can "borrow" his car.<P>I should have just stayed in bed.<P>Scott, I recall you mentioning almost needing a change of pants after your hvac system made a loud noise. Guess what it's like to approach a red light at 75mph, with no brakes. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-06-2000).]||||reg||13
Z||000008||Richard Parnell||01-06-2000||02:57 PM||||I did the ATF thing too.  The clutch is very firm and is slow coming back up.  The car shifts ok now, but when I put in the new master and slave I'm going to try the DOT4 or something similar, just to see if I can get it working without ATF.||||reg||11
Z||000009||fiero56||01-06-2000||03:15 PM||||I was just out in the driveway looking at my clutch situation. While pumping the clutch pedal, occasionaly I had to pull the pedal back off the floor. I know the springs are good, it's just those #$$% seals. I've cancelled my order through the Fiero Store and am going to bite the bullet and order another set of brand new master and slave cylinders. I'm not touching ATF with a 10' pole. That stuff isn't coming near my car again!||||reg||9
Z||000010||fiero56||01-06-2000||03:17 PM||||Hey, all x-ATF users. Lets band up and we can attack all the ATF crusaders <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. Monthey Python was on earlier this morning. I wish you guys that had bad ATF experiences had come forward when I was debating weather or not to use it. It would have really saved me some headache and $$$.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-06-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000011||Patrick||01-06-2000||04:23 PM||||<BR>(In the previous post, I was originally listed as the "head" of the ATF crusaders. I see that post has now been edited. I'm only mentioning this so that my post here continues to make sense. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P><B>fiero56</B>, I'm sure you're just kidding around, but let's get a couple of things straight. I wasn't the first person to suggest using an alternative to Dot3. And it wasn't me who originally suggested using ATF. I read about these things at this forum the same way you have. I questioned the use of ATF and was informed by others that there wouldn't be any problem. I had nothing to lose, so I gambled and it appears to have paid off for me. You gambled and it appears to have not worked out to your advantage.<P>Believe me, nobody is more sympathetic than I am when it comes to people having problems with the clutches in their Fieros. I went through two years of absolute hell with mine before I tried the ATF. It completely cured the problems I was having. I was only too happy to pass on my experiences in order to possibly help other people out. It's unfortunate that you still seem to be having problems. What more can I say?<p>[This message has been edited by Patrick (edited 01-17-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000012||fiero56||01-06-2000||06:39 PM||||Patrick,<BR>I by no means am blaming you, or anyone else. The information was there, I read it the same as everyone else, and decided to take a gamble at it. The only person I can blame is myself. There are no hard feelings coming from me. You took a gamble and won(which is great, considering how long you fought your clutch battles), I took a gamble and lost. I'm just kicking myself for not following factory recomendations. <BR>I do advise: if you want to try and save a faulty leaking mas. or slav. cylinder, try the ATF. There is nothing to lose. But, don't add ATF to a brand new cylinder as it just plain old swells those seals way too large. I just hope my experience helps someone else later on who is contemplating adding ATF to brand new cylinders.<BR>No hard feelings?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Patrick||01-06-2000||08:10 PM||||<BR>fiero56, I certainly don't have any hard feelings. I was just hoping you didn't. <P>If nothing else, your (and Alex4mula's) experience has shown that it may indeed be a bad idea to use ATF with slaves and masters that have new seals in them. My master was two years old and the slave was considerably older than that when I introduced ATF into my hydraulic system. <P>Of course, we're assuming that it is the AGE of the seals that results in the ATF swelling the seals exorbitantly or not. Maybe it's the BRAND of the (master & slave) seals and the particular kind of "rubber" that was used in their construction. It's possible that it does vary somewhat between manufacturers.<P>I just hope that you can get all your problems corrected without too many more headaches.||||reg||14
Z||000014||DRH||01-06-2000||08:34 PM||||Fiero56 (and others), Did you try flushing the ATF out and putting DOT3 back in? Might be too late, but it could save big $$$ if it's not and wouldn't take that long to try.<P>Patrick, other variables: <BR>- at least 2 designs of master and slave cylinder<BR>- what kind of ATF (Dextron II, III, ATF, ???) I know years ago Ford, GM and Mopar each had their own recommended auto trans fluid and the wrong one could cause seal problems.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Patrick||01-06-2000||09:06 PM||||<BR>DRH, good points!<P>I remember about a hundred years ago when I was working at my very first job out of high school as an air-cooled engine mechanic. Just for fun we used to soak different kinds of rubber objects in the bucket of really foul smelling carb-cleaner we had. It was amazing how large some of those things got. Interesting enough, the object would retain exactly its same shape. It wouldn't get all distorted, it would just get much larger. If I remember correctly, it seems that the objects would shrink down a bit when they were rinsed off in the varsol tank. The reason for this history lesson is that maybe there IS a chance that excessive swelling of the seals could be reversed.<BR> <BR>I'm aware of I believe SIX different kinds of ATF. There are synthetic and non-synthetic versions of Dextron II, Dextron III, and Type F. (Or has Dextron II been superseded by Dextron III?) There may also be others. I used non-synthetic Dextron III.||||reg||14
Z||000016||cobra427||01-06-2000||09:39 PM||||Hey guys, take this for what it is worth. I've read a lot of discussion on the clutch hydralic problems that many have had,on this forum and other forums as well.The overall concenses is,as one distinguised gentleman said,who used to work for Boeing,"If the parts we get ain't Boeing,it ain't going".I feel the same is true with both the electrics(pickup coil,module etc.)and clutch hydralics,(master,slave),to quote again,"If it ain't Delco,it won't go".  Sure the parts stores are cheaper but are they really if you want to factor in the aggravation factor.Spend a little more in the beginning and do it once in stead of making a hobby out making your clutch work.It seems that the remanufacturing process just isn't up to par. I know that some here are going to say how they bought one (master or slave unit)and it worked out just fine.My hat is off to you,but you are in the minority.Most have made several trips back to the parts store to return the slave or the master because it didn't work or only worked for a short while.Be good to yourselves and buy new Delco. Just my $.02<p>[This message has been edited by cobra427 (edited 01-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Patrick||01-06-2000||09:55 PM||||<BR>But cobra, we <B>enjoy</B> working on our cars... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000018||fiero56||01-06-2000||10:02 PM||||I put plain old non synthetic dexron III in my system as well. I used Raybestos parts. I agree with the ACdelco issue. I've heard numerouos peopl explain having problems with non O.E.M parts, ending up having to do the job a second time with the "proper" parts. I've heard the worst are non delco electrical parts. Fuel pumps, coil, etc.<P>When I put the ATF in my system, I used an air compressor to blow out all the old DOT3. I plan to do the same to get rid of the ATF before re-introducing brake fluid.<P>I pulled the leaking caliper. Turns out there is a small tear in one of the caliper sleeve boots. With my school/work schedule, it doesn't look like the Fiero is leaving the garage 'till the middle of next week. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||David||01-06-2000||10:06 PM||||Cobra427<BR>I had a new Delco on my Fiero and it was the worst of the three that I had on the car.  Lasted about 6 weeks before it gave me trouble.  I'm sorry to hear that some are having problems with ATF.  As for me, it cured my clutch problems, taking the poor operating Delco unit and making it perfect with nothing except a change of fluid.  I would do it again, if I only had a Fiero.   <p>[This message has been edited by David (edited 01-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||2birds||01-06-2000||10:30 PM||||I agree with Cobra427 on the electric parts.  I'll use stuff from aftermarket suppliers only if it's an upgrade, such as Raybestos/Bendix/Moog. I've used parts store brands with mostly poor results. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||cobra427||01-06-2000||11:32 PM||||Dave,<BR>You can't get perfection from imperfection. Even the best can be flawed at times,(Delco,Raybestor,Bendix,etc.),but I think that the odds are in your favor when you choose to buy OEM or proven name brands.<P>We all like to work on our gems,but I don't think any of us like to work on the things that cause us great frustration tho.The fun is ,in my opinion,doing the upgrades that make our cars work just alittle better than when we got them or just the regular maintainence that it will take to try and reach the magical marks of 200,000 or 300,000. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||2FAST4U||01-07-2000||12:16 AM||||The difference between Dextron Mercon and Type F have nothing to do with the effect on seals. The difference and the reason they are not interchangable is due to the lubricity values and clutch pack slippage.||||reg||1
Z||000023||fiero56||01-16-2000||09:07 PM||||I took my "old" master and slave cylinders out last night. WOW!!! I have to stand on them to push the piston down, and it doesn't come back up. I took the air compressor as high as I dared(100psi), hooked up the hose to each cylinder, and the pistons wouldn't budge. I don't think it's possible to get them out because of the swelled seals. I'm actually surprised the rod exteding from the master never bent while driving the car.<BR>I cleared the line of all remaining ATF and will be finishing the clutch portion of my project later tonight. I'll let everyone know if the problem is cured later in the week after I get some miles on the cylinders.<P>Don't put ATF in brand new cylinders is my only warning. I have two brand new cylinders to show as proof.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Cooter||01-16-2000||09:25 PM||||Dont bother with using jack oil either. It worked great for a few weeks, but then the cylinders began to stick because the seals had expanded to over twice their normal size. It took forever to get the cylinders apart. Now I have rebuilt the cylinders and filled the system with synthetic brake fluid. I will hope this lasts longer than the previous attempt.||||reg||1
Z||000025||lowCG||01-16-2000||10:54 PM||||Well if a large aircraft company can order parts from everywhere(they do)then so can anyone for their car,just have to make sure,somehow,that what you're getting is alright and up to the task.The dealer tried to sell me some ball joints awhile back that never had anything to do with their supply chain,but instead were marked up from the local auto parts store.What it sounds like happened to the seals was a result of some sort of reaction between the ATF and normal brake fluid,which could've been used as an assembly lube.An old used car dealer's trick to make an automatic shift better is to add a little brake fluid to the tranny to swell up the seals a bit.If you rebuild your own hydraulics and clean well,the ATF should work fine.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Patrick||01-17-2000||12:07 AM||||<BR>I guess the gods must have taken mercy on me, because when I switched over to <B>ATF</B>, I did NOT make any special effort to clean the old DOT3 out of the system. I simply emptied the master cylinder reservoir before I added the ATF, and then I bled the system until the red ATF came out of the bleeder valve.<P>It's really unfortunate that several people have reported problems with the seals swelling up so badly. We've discussed this before, but there must be some rational reason(s) why this procedure has been a blessing for some and a curse for others.||||reg||3
Z||000027||TOM||01-17-2000||12:20 AM||||I told afew way back when not to use atf in the clutch system. ||||reg||1
Z||000029||mwbackus||01-17-2000||01:41 AM||||As I said in a post last month, "the seal material used for brake fluid systems (Ethylene Propylene) is incompatible with petroleum based fluids".  Very worn seals that have hardened over time may seem to work better, but they will "eventually" decompose.  This is not just my opinion, it is fact based on chemical compatibility data, available to anyone who wishes to research it.<P>Mike <BR>(ACS Certified Chemist - retired)||||reg||1
Z||000030||1FST2M6||01-17-2000||11:05 AM||||I had issues with the exhaust of my car cooking the DOT 3 and DOT 4 fluid. Knowing what ATF will do to the floor of the garage i didn't even think of messing with it in a sealed poly ring pressurized enclouser. I went to the local Audi/Porsche dealer and got some Agip DOT 5.1 brake fluid. it was about $18(US) for a quart and haven't had any problems with either direction of the clutch pedal. Its been in there for about 4000 miles. I later found out that NAPA carrys a DOT 5.1 at about $15 not sure of the name but its in a round white plastic bottle (and its pretty and blue!) ||||reg||1
Z||000031||mutfish||01-17-2000||03:17 PM||||I too replaced the fluid in my new system with atf last summer....read this thread earlier and went out to check the pedal in my "stored for the winter'88 GT. I'll be a son of a b***h!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000032||FieroSTETZ||01-18-2000||01:56 PM||||eh.. i just replaced my master cylinder, loaded it with ATF after reading everyones positive commentary, then it pooped out on me. the clutch pedal went down, stayed there, and now it moves freely- it feels like the rubber thingy or whatever was on the endo of the rod came loose and is just sitting in there. my car will not go into gear at all now. can someone tell me how to repair the new master cylinder? i spent 3 weeks saving up the 112 dollars for the new master..||||reg||1
Z||000033||1FST2M6||01-18-2000||02:14 PM||||Napa carrys a Rebuild kit for both the slave  and the master cyl. purchase this kit and a brake caliper hone. Or just clean it up REALLY good and say its defective and return it for another. Use DOT 5.1 Brake fluid next time!! It can take the heat and will NOT eat ANY o-rings or rubberliner in the braided feed line to the slave. It may be expensive but you only have to do it once! Since its a fully synthetic liquid there is nothing petroleum based in the mix to mess with anything! You guy might want to look at the feed line to the slave and make sure it too it not on the verge of failing! And make sure you flush the lines BEFORE you hook it all back up... not sure how you can do that but even trace amounts of it in your newley replaced expensive parts system will cause it to fail prematurely. oh and Listen to MWBACKUS too I think he out of all of us would have the best clue!!<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||fiero56||01-18-2000||02:24 PM||||I don't want to insult you, but make sure the rod extending from the master is still connected to the clutch pedal. There is a clip that holds the rod on the pedal. It may have slipped off alowing the rod to fall off the pedal. Which would allow the pedal to be completly "dead". <BR>Since there is no resistance I would check, all clutch line connections, no leaking coming from slave, fluid level, and the master cyl rod. Also make sure the rod extending from the slave is still connected at both ends. I can't see bad seals allowing a pedal to go completely dead, plus there is a spring in the master that would push the pedal up a little bit. I think something isn't right with your m.c. connection.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000035||1FST2M6||01-18-2000||02:34 PM||||Another fix!!  If the bushing that Fiero56 (between the master and the pedal) was talking about becomes lost damaged or stolen Home Depot/Ace hardware all carry a similar snap bushing (if you can't get another one fron the dealer)HOWEVER it will not stay in place like the factory piece. You must retain this adapted piece with a 1/4" E-clip. I did that and haven't had a problem in over 6000 miles. Total cost... about $.60<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000036||fiero56||01-18-2000||07:50 PM||||My clutch system is now running with dot3 and what a difference. I bought another set of brand new master and slave cylinders, bled all the ATF out, and am now running dot3. <BR>Pedal feel is very light, almost "air in the system" springy. For the first time, I love my new RAM clutch set more than the stock clutch. Pedal feel is light, shifting is smooth an quick, and enguagement is very smooth.<BR>When I ran ATF, I almost had to stand on the pedal, clutch enguagement was very sloppy, and the pedal never wanted to leave the floor. DOT3 is the way to be!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I now love to drive my Fiero again. <BR>I promise to never ignore factory recommendations again. Too much money and headache involved. Just though someone might want to know the DOT3 was best, and my RAM clutch is very nice, I would buy one again.||||reg||1
Z||000037||FieroSTETZ||01-18-2000||09:57 PM||||the rod is still connected and everything... there is only some pedal resistance about .5 inches before the pedal hits the floor. like i said, it feels like whatever is on the end of the rod to push into the cylinder has come off or something. thanks for the responses!||||reg||1
Z||000039||Patrick||01-18-2000||11:24 PM||||<BR>I don't know if my comments are welcome in this thread any more but... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><B>FieroSTETZ</B>, if I remember correctly, the rod from the clutch pedal to the master cylinder is actually held in place (and prevented from falling out of the cylinder) by a clip. It's possible that if the piston is stuck at the far end of the cylinder that the clutch pedal would feel like what you're describing. Keep in mind that the rod is NOT connected to the piston, it just pushes against it. It's the piston (supposedly) returning to it's rest position that pushes the rod (and clutch pedal) back out.||||reg||9
Z||000040||Kage86GT4SP||01-19-2000||02:32 AM||||Well, I have to add my .02.<P>I replaced what seems to have been the OEM fluid in my clutch system with Dex III ATF fluid two months (and 4K) ago.  The difference has been amazing.<P>I don't believe it was synthetic... it was, I think (I don't have it here in Olympia with me   in the middle of the legislative session... I'll check it out this weekend back home) Havoline or Valvoline.<P>I haven't lost a drop, and the shifting action is great.  The clutch is out with a very short throw, and shifting is truly enjoyable.<P>And the controversy continues....<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000041||fiero56||01-19-2000||08:02 AM||||Fierostetz:<BR>I just re-read your post, and I know the problem, thanks to Patrick who got me thinking again.<BR>When I pulled my master the piston was where it belonged, pushed against the clip that holds it in. The seals were so tight, that when I pushed the rod down, the piston stayed bottomed out, because the p.plate was no longer forcing the piston back up. I was able to take 120psi and after a good 60 seconds the piston finally shot out of the cylinder. You need a rebuild kit. All that is happening right now is the rod is moving freely in the bore, but there is no resistance since the piston is bottomed out.||||reg||1
Z||000042||mutfish||01-19-2000||12:54 PM||||I just removed the master and slave cylinders,and sure enough the pistons were pretty much stuck.Both were new when I used the atf.I took them apart, cleaned everything and reassembeled them both using DOT 5 silicone fluid.They seem to be free moving now,with no sticking.I've decided DOT 5 is all that's going in the clutch system from now on.I'ts been in my corvette's brake system for over 6 years without a hitch.....||||reg||1
Z||000043||Tom88gt||01-19-2000||01:16 PM||||ATF...DOT 5...ATF...DOT 5<P>Has anyone tried DOT 4?  It is compatible with DOT 3 and is supposedly not as hydroscopic?  Wouldn't this solve a lot of the DOT 3 problems, not cause the bushings to swelling from ATF, eliminate replacing all rubber for DOT 5?  <P>Costs less than DOT 5 but more than DOT 3.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000044||Patrick||01-19-2000||02:42 PM||||<BR><B>mutfish</B>, did you use the same seals when you cleaned up and reassembled your cylinders? If so, that's great to hear! That's what I was hoping when I made my <B>01-06-2000 09:06 PM</B> post. <P>I'm sure it's still a hassle, but at least it's a lot cheaper than having to buy new parts.    ||||reg||5
Z||000045||1FST2M6||01-19-2000||02:59 PM||||I had an issue with my clutch not working properly after i put the DOT 5.1 in and we checked everything...  then realized that the release fork was bent and would just BARELY move the pressure plate.. It was in stock at NAPA for $70-$80 very nice piece. much nicer than the factory piece. Not tack welded but press fit and fully welded, mild steel! They even had the bushing i beat the crap out of trying to get it out.||||reg||1
Z||000046||mutfish||01-19-2000||03:26 PM||||Patrick,Yes used the same seals,they were in contact with the atf for less than 60 days, so maybe that saved them.Plus they were brand new.I have yet to put them back on the car, but I bench bled the master and it seems ok....if anything it might work a little better than new!||||reg||1
Z||000048||Patrick||01-19-2000||04:47 PM||||<BR><B>mutfish</B>, that sounds really promising. There's a bunch of us out here who have our fingers crossed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I should have mentioned in my previous post that it was David (DRH) who first suggested simply reintroducing DOTx back into the system after flushing the ATF out. It was his suggestion that reactivated the memories I had from years ago about swelling up "rubber" objects in the carb cleaner and shrinking them down again in the varsol tank. ||||reg||14
Z||000049||fiero56||01-19-2000||07:26 PM||||I've been "massaging" the seals for my master cylinder with DOT3. Still no go. After I got them out, they won't even fit in the larger opening before the cylinder bore begins. Mine were messed up for quite some time before pulling them. The seals exposed to ATF are at least double the thickness than the DOT3 exposed ones, plus the ATF seals flare out more. It may depend on how long the seals were exposed to the ATF, mine had 1,000 miles on them.||||reg||1
Z||000050||theogre||01-20-2000||07:43 PM||||You know,,,, I should have remembered this problem. must be the heavy sedatives........ or senility......<P>I tried to store a brake master cyl years ago. to keep it from rusting I put some oil in it. latter it was trashed. I had completely forgotten about it and how it's seals hated oil. At least it didn't rust. The theory behind the clutch mod is sound, but the materials don't like it. nuts.<P>When you are flushing the line make sure you use a "dry" flush. I.E. you can't use allot of store alcohol. nearly all have water in them. You have to use 100% isopropyl or something similar.<P>You also have to make sure your air line has a dryer on it.<P>In this case we're not so worried about water lowering the boiling point of the DOT 3. We're worried about corrosion.<P>Even then I'd still flush most of a quart bottle of fluid thru to carry out moisture. You cant see water in brake fluid.||||reg||1
Z||000051||Phil||01-20-2000||07:52 PM||||How about acetone?||||reg||1
Z||000052||fiero56||01-20-2000||08:05 PM||||While bleeding the clutch system with DOT3, I went through an entire 32oz bottle. Hope thats enough to flush out any water, no I didn't think to put a dryer on the line(humidity was down though <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).||||reg||1
Z||000053||theogre||01-21-2000||08:29 PM||||I'd stay with alcohol. It will evaporate quickly. Acetone isn't as fast to dry as I recall. With something that drys fast you have less risk of contaminating the new fluid.||||reg||1
Z||000054||DRA||01-22-2000||01:36 AM||||Tried the ATF with no luck, flushed the system and reintroduced DOT3 brake fluid, its like new now. May make more since just to flush the system to replace the 12+ year old brake fluid. There has to be some break down of the fluid, moisture, etc, over the years. Mechanic friend of mine freaked when I told him that I had put ATF in the clutch, said it would turn the seals to jelly. ||||reg||1
